Durarara!! AU Chapter 2 – Part 1
Rio eventually let go.
She stepped back, wiped at her face once with the sleeve of her shirt, and gave Curtis a small, unsteady nod. No more words. Just that. Then she turned and started walking, shoulders still a little hunched, but her feet moving forward instead of toward another rooftop.
Curtis watched her go until she reached the corner and disappeared from sight.
Only then did he notice the other presence.
A woman in black leather had approached without making a sound. She wore a full-face yellow helmet with small cat-ear accents on top, the visor dark enough that nothing of her face could be seen. The leather suit fit close, practical, built for riding. She stopped a few paces away and simply stood there, the empty space where a head should have been somehow still managing to feel directed at him.
Rio had walked right past her without slowing down.
The woman lifted her phone and typed with practiced speed. she turned the phone to him
“You saved that girl.”
She didn’t move closer. Instead her helmet turned slightly toward the side of the building. The spiderweb cracks Curtis had left in the concrete were still visible, pale and sharp under the streetlights. Her attention shifted downward next, to the fractured pavement where he had landed with Rio in his arms.
Curtis studied her in silence.
The black leather. The yellow helmet. The way she stood like the shadows themselves had decided to take a shape and walk. He took in the lack of any visible breath, the absolute stillness of her posture, the faint residual trace of something dark that had already pulled back into the alley behind her.
He didn’t speak right away. He simply looked at her the way he looked at most new things in this city—calm, measuring, and without the usual human need to fill the quiet.
Curtis kept his eyes on the yellow helmet.
“And?” he said, voice low and even behind the mask. “It seems you were going to do the same.”
The woman in black leather didn’t shift her weight or make any unnecessary movement. She simply lifted the PDA again and typed with quick, precise taps. The artificial voice that followed was the same flat, clear tone as before.
“Yes. I was.”
A short pause, then more typing.
“I saw her on the edge. I was already moving when you reached her first. You were faster.”
The helmet tilted slightly toward the cracked wall, then down to the fractured pavement at Curtis’s feet. Another few taps.
“Those cracks… normal people cannot do that. The wall, the ground. You took the fall and the girl without injury. That is not ordinary strength.”
She lowered the Phone a fraction, the dark visor of the yellow helmet still fixed on him.
“I do not know what you are. But you saved her. That is enough for me not to interfere further tonight.”
Celty remained still, waiting to see whether the tall, masked man would offer anything else—or simply walk away the same way the girl had.
Celty remained still for a few seconds.
Then, unexpectedly, she spoke.
The womens voice that came out from beneath the yellow helmet was quiet,and carried a faint, uncertain edge—like someone testing whether the words would even work.
“…What are you?”
She tilted the helmet slightly, the dark visor still locked on Curtis. The question wasn’t hostile. It sounded closer to genuine confusion mixed with caution.
“I’ve seen a lot of things in this city. People with strength. People who aren’t entirely human. But the way you moved… the cracks in the wall… catching her like that without even bracing…”
A short pause.
“You didn’t hesitate. Most people freeze. Or look away. You just… decided she wasn’t going to hit the ground.”
Celty’s gloved fingers flexed once at her side.
“You’re not from around here. That much is obvious. And you’re hiding your face for a reason. I don’t care about that. But I do care about what walks through Ikebukuro at night when it can do what you just did.”
She fell quiet again, the yellow helmet angled toward him, waiting.
The phone stayed at her side this time. For once, the Headless Rider had chosen to speak with her own.
Curtis answered without raising his voice.
“I hide my face because I feel like it. No one’s after me. And I won’t stand by while someone tries to kill themselves.”
The yellow helmet stayed locked on him.
For several seconds Celty didn’t move at all. Her gloved fingers hovered over the Phone screen, frozen mid-motion.She hadn’t typed a single word of the question that had just been in her mind.
Yet he had answered it.
A sharp, almost startled tension ran through her shoulders. The Headless Rider took half a step back before she caught herself. In all the years she had spent in Ikebukuro, no one had ever responded to something she hadn’t put through the PDA. People couldn’t hear her. That was the entire reason the device existed.
She stared at the masked man in front of her, the dark visor of the yellow helmet giving nothing away while her thoughts raced.
He heard me.
Slowly, carefully, she lifted the Phone and typed with deliberate precision. The artificial voice finally spoke, flatter than usual.
“…How did you know what I was going to ask?”
The question hung in the night air between them. Celty remained completely still, waiting, the cracks in the wall and pavement suddenly feeling a lot more significant than they had a minute ago.
Curtis tilted his head slightly, the black lenses of his sunglasses reflecting the streetlights.
“I didn’t?” he said, voice calm behind the mask. “I can hear you.”
Celty went completely still.
The PDA in her hand remained silent for several long seconds. Her gloved fingers didn’t move. The yellow helmet stayed fixed on him as if the empty space beneath it was trying to process what he had just said.
No one could hear her.
That was one of the most basic, unchanging facts of her existence. Without a head, without a voice, the only way she had ever been able to communicate was through the device. People reacted to the artificial voice. They never reacted to her.
Until now.
Slowly, she raised the PDA again. The taps on the screen were sharper this time, less measured. The artificial voice that came out carried none of its usual neutral tone—it almost sounded clipped.
“That is impossible.”
A short pause. More typing.
“No one can hear me. I do not have a voice. That is why I use this. You should not be able to hear anything from me unless it comes through the speaker.”
The helmet tilted a fraction, the dark visor locked on the masked man in front of her.
“What are you?”
Curtis moved before she could type anything else.
His hand came up, steady and unhurried, and gently but firmly pressed the PDA downward until it was no longer raised between them. He didn’t snatch it. He simply lowered it, the way someone might move an obstacle out of the way of a conversation.
The black lenses stayed fixed on the yellow helmet.
“Say something again,” he said.
Celty froze.
Her gloved fingers tightened around the device the moment it left its usual height. For a headless Dullahan who had spent years relying on that little screen to be understood, the sudden loss of it felt almost like having a limb pinned. The dark visor of the yellow helmet tilted sharply toward his hand, then back up to his face.
She didn’t try to raise the PDA again right away. She just stood there, the black leather of her suit unmoving, the empty space beneath the helmet somehow radiating pure, stunned stillness.
No one had ever done that.
No one had ever told her to speak.
The night air between them felt heavier than it had a moment ago. The cracks in the wall and the pavement seemed to sit in the background like quiet proof that the man in front of her was already breaking rules she thought were fixed.
Celty’s free hand curled slowly at her side.
She still didn’t type.
For a long moment Celty didn’t move.
Then, very quietly, a voice came from beneath the yellow helmet.
It was soft. Rough around the edges, like something that hadn’t been used in a very long time—or like something that wasn’t supposed to exist at all.
“…How can you hear me?”
The words were barely above a whisper, but they were clear. Real. Not the flat artificial tone of the PDA. Her own.
Curtis didn’t flinch. He didn’t look surprised. He simply kept his hand lightly resting on the lowered PDA, black lenses steady on the helmet in front of him.
“I told you,” he said, voice even. “I can hear you.”
He let the silence sit for a beat before continuing, calm and direct.
“You don’t need that thing with me. If you’ve got something to say… just say it.”
He released the PDA and let his hand drop back to his side, giving her the choice to raise it again or leave it down.
The yellow helmet remained completely still. The night around them felt like it was holding its breath
Celty Sturluson stood frozen on the cracked sidewalk.
The yellow helmet remained perfectly still, but beneath it her thoughts were moving too fast. For the first time in longer than she could cleanly remember, a sound that belonged to her had left the space where her head should have been—and someone had answered it.
Not the PDA.
Not the artificial voice she had relied on for years.
Her.
She had spoken, and the tall man in the gray hoodie and black mask had heard her without hesitation.
The weight of that fact settled over her like a second skin. No one in Ikebukuro had ever done this. No one in this country. No one since she had lost her head. The PDA had been her only bridge to the living world. Now a complete stranger had simply… stepped past it.
Slowly, almost carefully, she tried again.
“…You can really hear me.”
It wasn’t a question this time. The words came out quieter than before, still rough, still uncertain, but unmistakably hers. She watched him through the dark visor, waiting to see if the impossible would happen a second time.
Curtis remained exactly where he was, hands at his sides, black lenses reflecting the streetlights. He gave a small, single nod.
“Yeah.”
That was all.
Celty’s gloved fingers tightened around the PDA she still held at her side. She didn’t raise it. For the first time in a long while, she didn’t feel the immediate need to.
The Headless Rider had met someone who could hear her.
And she had no idea what that meant yet.
Curtis gave a small shrug, the heavy gray hoodie shifting with the motion.
“Why is that so surprising?” he asked, voice still calm behind the mask. “Anyway, if we’re done here, I’ve got more sights to see before the last train.”
He said it like the entire conversation—the cracked wall, the falling girl, the fact that he could hear a voice no one else should have been able to hear—had been nothing more than a brief delay in his night.
Celty didn’t move right away.
The yellow helmet stayed angled toward him, the dark visor unreadable. Her gloved hand was still closed around the PDA at her side, but she made no attempt to lift it. For a few seconds she simply stood there, processing the casual way he was already preparing to leave.
Most people, once they realized something was wrong with her, either stared too long, asked too many questions, or backed away. This one had heard her voice, acknowledged it, and was now ready to keep walking like it was only mildly interesting.
She finally spoke again, the rough, quiet voice emerging from beneath the helmet once more.
“…You’re just going to leave?”
Curtis had already half-turned toward the street. He glanced back at her over his shoulder, black lenses catching the light.
“Unless you’ve got something else to say.”
Curtis didn’t wait for an answer.
He turned fully away from her and started walking, hands sliding back into the front pocket of the gray hoodie. The black mask, sunglasses, and Jumpman cap stayed exactly where they were. His steps were unhurried, the same steady pace he’d had all night—like catching a falling girl and speaking with a headless rider were just two more things that had happened on his first evening in Ikebukuro.
The cracked pavement and spiderwebbed wall faded behind him with every step.
Celty remained where she stood, yellow helmet turned toward his retreating back. The PDA stayed silent at her side. She didn’t call out. She didn’t type. She simply watched the tall, masked figure move deeper into the neon-lit streets, the night air settling around the space he had left empty.
For a moment the Headless Rider said nothing at all.
Then, very quietly, almost to herself—
“…What are you?”
But Curtis was already too far to answer, even if he could still hear her.
Curtis had only made it another two blocks when a voice cut across his path.
“Well now. That’s an interesting face to be hiding.”
A young man stepped out from the shadow of a building, blocking the sidewalk with the easy confidence of someone who had never once worried about being in the way. He was lean, sharp-eyed, and dressed in a black coat with a thick white fur collar that stood out even under the neon. A knowing little smile sat on his face like it belonged there permanently.
Izaya Orihara tilted his head, studying the tall figure in the gray hoodie, black mask, sunglasses, and Jumpman cap.
“You’re the one who left those cracks in the wall earlier, aren’t you?” he said lightly. “And the girl. Rio Kamichika. She’s still walking around instead of decorating the pavement, so I suppose I should say thank you… or at least hello.”
He didn’t move aside. He simply stood there, hands in his coat pockets, smiling like he had all the time in the world and had already decided this particular masked stranger was worth a closer look.
Curtis came to a stop.
Behind the black lenses, his gaze settled on the newcomer without any visible change in expression. He didn’t speak right away. He simply waited, the same quiet, measuring stillness he had shown Celty.
Izaya’s smile widened a fraction.
“Don’t be shy. I don’t bite. Much.” He gave a small, theatrical bow of the head. “Izaya Orihara. And you are…?”
Curtis didn’t answer the question.
He stood there in silence for a moment, black lenses fixed on the young man in the fur-collared coat. When he finally spoke, his voice was low and even behind the mask.
“You’re the boy I saw on the roof with Rio.”
It wasn’t a question.
Izaya’s smile didn’t fade, but something sharper flickered behind his eyes for just a second. He rocked back on his heels slightly, still blocking the sidewalk, and let out a soft, amused little sound.
“Ah. So you did notice me up there.” He tilted his head, the white fur of his collar shifting with the movement. “Most people are a little too busy panicking—or falling—to pay attention to the scenery. You’re observant. I like that.”
He made no attempt to deny it.
Instead, Izaya took a small step closer, still smiling, as if the accusation were nothing more than an interesting conversation starter.
“She told you what I said, didn’t she?” he asked lightly. “About her reason being pathetic. Harsh of me, I know. But people are so much more entertaining when you give them a little push and see which way they fall.”
His gaze flicked over Curtis’s masked face, the cracked strength still lingering in the air between them from earlier.
“And then you showed up. Caught her. Left those lovely spiderweb cracks in my city. Very dramatic.” Izaya’s smile widened. “So tell me, mysterious stranger who won’t give his name… what exactly are you doing in Ikebukuro?”
The low, familiar growl of an engine cut through the night before either of them could speak again.
A black motorcycle rolled up to the curb a few meters away, engine settling into a deep idle. The rider was dressed in the same black leather as before, yellow cat-eared helmet still in place. Celty Sturluson planted one boot on the pavement and killed the engine, the sudden quiet making the street feel sharper.
She didn’t get off the bike right away. She simply sat there, dark visor angled toward the two figures on the sidewalk—the tall masked man in the gray hoodie, and the lean young man in the fur-collared coat who was smiling like he owned the conversation.
Izaya’s expression brightened the moment he saw her.
“Celty,” he called cheerfully, raising one hand in a lazy wave. “What perfect timing. I was just getting acquainted with our new friend here. The one who leaves cracks in concrete and catches falling girls.”
Celty’s helmet turned slowly from Izaya to Curtis, then back again. One gloved hand rested on the handlebar. The other stayed near her PDA, though she didn’t lift it yet.
The Headless Rider had followed the masked stranger after all.
And now she had found him standing face-to-face with the one person in Ikebukuro she trusted least.
Celty’s real voice came first—quiet, sharp, and meant for only one person on the street.
“Don’t tell him you can hear me.”
The words never reached Izaya. They went straight to Curtis, low and urgent beneath the yellow helmet.
Only then did she lift the PDA.
Her gloved fingers moved quickly across the screen. The artificial voice spoke a moment later, flat and clear in the night air for both of them to hear.
“Why did you make her jump?”
The question was aimed directly at Izaya.
Izaya’s smile didn’t falter, but his eyes lit up with fresh interest as he looked between the Headless Rider and the masked man standing across from him. He slipped his hands deeper into his coat pockets, rocking slightly on his heels like the entire situation was entertainment made just for him.
“Make her jump?” he repeated, all innocence. “Celty, you wound me. I simply had a conversation with the girl. She already wanted to do it. I just… helped her clarify her feelings.”
His gaze slid sideways to Curtis, curious and amused.
“Though I notice someone decided to interfere. Very heroic. Very sudden. And now here you are again, Celty, rolling up like a worried guardian. How touching.”
He tilted his head, still smiling.
“So tell me—are the two of you already acquainted? Because this is starting to look like a very interesting night.”
Curtis gave a small shrug, the heavy fabric of the gray hoodie shifting with the movement.
“Nope. I just got here,” he said, voice even behind the mask. “I’m not too keen on giving my name to two strangers in the dark.”
Izaya let out a short, delighted little laugh.
“Oh, I like him already.” He glanced at Celty as if sharing a private joke, then looked back at the masked figure. “Cautious. Sensible. Mysterious. You’re checking all the interesting boxes tonight. First you leave spiderweb cracks in my city, then you refuse to introduce yourself… it’s almost rude.”
He didn’t sound offended. If anything, he sounded more entertained than before.
Celty remained on the motorcycle, yellow helmet angled toward Curtis. She didn’t type anything right away. The fact that he had kept their private exchange to himself—that he hadn’t casually revealed he could hear her real voice—seemed to settle something in her posture. She stayed quiet, watching.
Izaya took a half-step closer, still smiling that sharp, curious smile.
“Fine, no name then. I’ll just have to get creative.” He tilted his head. “Masked foreigner. Tall. Strong enough to crack concrete. Shows up on the same night a girl almost dies and a Dullahan takes an interest. You’re making my evening very complicated… and I do love complicated.”
His eyes glittered under the streetlights.
“So if you won’t tell me who you are… at least tell me what you plan to do in Ikebukuro.”
Curtis’s voice stayed low and even behind the mask.
“Interesting. I’m rude… yet Rio told me you were supposed to kill yourself with her. Why didn’t you? To prove a point? That’s dangerous — playing with human lives. In contrast to someone who doesn’t want to give their name.”
The words settled over the sidewalk like a quiet weight.
Izaya’s smile didn’t disappear, but it changed. The playful edge sharpened into something thinner and more attentive. For a brief moment the usual theatrics dropped just enough to show that the masked stranger had actually landed a clean hit.
He let out a soft, almost appreciative breath.
“Ohhh. She told you that part too.” Izaya’s eyes narrowed with genuine interest. “You’re not just strong and mysterious… you’re thorough. I really like that.”
He rocked back on his heels, hands still buried in the pockets of his fur-collared coat, and regarded Curtis like a new puzzle that had just become significantly more entertaining.
“You’re right, of course. I didn’t jump. I never planned to. The girl needed a little… clarity. People reveal the most interesting things about themselves when they’re standing on the edge. I simply provided the opportunity.”
His gaze flicked briefly toward Celty, then back to the black lenses in front of him.
“And now here you are, lecturing me about the value of human life while refusing to show your face or give your name. How very principled of you.” The smile returned, wider this time. “Tell me something, mysterious foreigner — do you always get this involved on your first night in a new city… or am I just special?”
Curtis exhaled quietly behind the mask, the sound almost like a sigh.
“Sooo… any reason for this?” he asked, voice still calm. “If not, I’ma be on my way.”
He shifted his weight slightly, already half-turned toward the open street, making it clear the conversation had reached the end of his patience. The black lenses stayed on Izaya for a second longer, waiting to see if the other man would actually give him a reason to stay.
Izaya’s smile widened, delighted by the blunt dismissal.
“Straight to the point. I respect it.” He didn’t move out of the way yet. “No grand reason, I suppose. I simply don’t often meet someone who can crack concrete, catch a falling girl, and lecture me about morality all in the same night. You’re a rare type. I was hoping to understand you a little better before you vanished into the city.”
He glanced sideways at Celty, who still sat on her motorcycle in silence, yellow helmet angled toward the exchange.
“But if you’re determined to leave…” Izaya stepped aside with a small, theatrical flourish of one hand, clearing the sidewalk. “Far be it from me to keep a man from the last train. Just know that Ikebukuro has a way of pulling interesting people back into its orbit.”
His eyes glittered with quiet amusement.
“I’ll be seeing you around, nameless stranger.”
Celty remained still on the bike, saying nothing — neither with her real voice nor the PDA. She simply watched as Curtis prepared to walk away for the second time that night.
Curtis’s footsteps faded down the street, the tall figure in the gray hoodie disappearing into the neon without looking back.
Only once he was out of easy earshot did Celty finally turn her helmet fully toward Izaya.
She lifted the PDA. The artificial voice came out flat and cold.
“You pushed that girl.”
Izaya’s smile remained perfectly in place. He watched the direction Curtis had gone for a moment longer before turning his attention back to the Headless Rider, eyes bright with residual amusement.
“I had a conversation with her,” he corrected lightly. “She was already standing on the edge. I simply didn’t drag her back from it. There’s a difference.”
Celty’s gloved fingers moved again.
“You made a suicide pact with her and then laughed when she told you her reason. That is not a conversation.”
“Details, details.” Izaya waved a hand dismissively, though his gaze flicked once more toward the street where the masked man had vanished. “I’m far more interested in him right now. Did you see the way he looked at me when he realized I was the one on the roof? No fear. No hesitation. Just quiet judgment. And those cracks in the wall… that wasn’t normal human strength.”
He tilted his head, studying Celty.
“You’ve been unusually quiet. And you showed up awfully fast after he left you earlier. Don’t tell me our mysterious foreigner already made an impression on the great Celty Sturluson.”
The yellow helmet remained still for a few seconds.
Then the PDA spoke again, the tone as neutral as ever.
“He is dangerous. And you are playing with things you do not understand… as usual.”
Izaya’s smile only grew sharper.
“Oh, Celty. That’s what makes it fun.”
Celty watched Izaya for a moment longer, the yellow helmet unreadable.
She didn’t bother typing anything else. There was nothing left worth saying to him. Instead she turned the key, and the black motorcycle growled back to life beneath her. Izaya called something after her—light, mocking, already moving on to the next amusement—but the words were lost under the engine as she pulled away from the curb.
The Headless Rider cut through the side streets of Ikebukuro with practiced ease, black leather and yellow helmet blending into the night traffic. She wasn’t following the main roads. She was tracking the path the tall figure in the gray hoodie had taken after leaving them.
Curtis hadn’t been moving fast. He walked like someone who had nowhere urgent to be and no one waiting for him. That made him easier to keep in sight from a distance.
Celty stayed far enough back that the sound of her engine wouldn’t immediately give her away. Every so often she slowed at a corner, helmet turning as she searched the sidewalks and crosswalks ahead. The masked foreigner who could hear her voice—who had cracked concrete and refused to give his name—had already proven he was not ordinary.
She wanted to know where someone like that went on their first night in the city.
The neon signs reflected off her dark visor as she continued after him, silent except for the low rumble of the motorcycle, determined not to lose the trail.